{"id": "sevt_01VgXpmKryWAUxYcXGijWWg6", "content": [{"text": "Checkout p99 was already climbing before the 14:30 spike, starting right after the 14:06 deploy of `371dfe2`. That deploy is the most likely cause. The 14:29 cliff itself is only partly explained, as noted below.\n\n- **Deploys:** `371dfe2` (checkout, \"refactor order summary builder\") went out at 14:06:20, and p99 began rising at 14:06. `db46df5` (cache disable) was live from 14:18 to 14:23 and was rolled back. It doesn't line up with the cliff, which came about 6 minutes after the rollback.\n- **p99 trend:** It rose steadily from about 75 ms at 14:05 to about 410 ms at 14:28. It then jumped to 2,152 ms at 14:29 and about 4,000 ms or more from 14:30.\n- **DB pool:** Checkout `db_pool_utilization` climbed in step with p99, from 0.14 at 14:06 to 0.76 at 14:29. It saturated at 1.0 from 14:38 onward.\n- **Diff:** `371dfe2` replaced one batched `order_id__in` query plus in-memory grouping with a per-order `OrderItem.objects.filter(order_id=...)` inside the loop. That is an N+1 query pattern.\n- **Log:** There are 3,770 per-row `SELECT * FROM order_items WHERE order_id = ?` queries, the first at 14:06:21. I saw no pool or timeout errors in the 14:29\u201314:30 window, only routine GC pauses.\n\nI didn't pull checkout's `error_rate`. I also didn't check what traffic or order sizes were at 14:29, so I can't say why the jump came then rather than gradually. My best guess is that load crossed a threshold as the pool neared saturation. Rolling back `371dfe2` (restoring the batched query) is the fix to try first. Confidence is moderate-high on the cause and lower on why the cliff came at 14:29.\n\n**Root cause:** `371dfe2` \u2014 the refactor replaced a single batched `order_items` query with one query per order inside the loop, which steadily exhausted checkout's DB connection pool and drove p99 latency up to the spike.", "type": "text"}], "processed_at": "2026-10-14T11:36:17.592824Z", "type": "agent.message"}
